        <body>
            <div xml:id="d49">

                <pb facs="0161" n="55" sameAs="frus:frus1891/pg55"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Kimberly to Mr. Blaine</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States in Central America</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1891-02-04"><hi rendition="#italic">February
                                4, 1891</hi>.</date> (Received February 20).</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 246.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my No. 243, of January 31, I
                    have the honor to report that to-day Señor Prado, acting minister of foreign
                    relations, in Señor Don F. Anguiano’s absence, called at this legation, and,
                    calling my attention to the instructions to Capt. Johnston, of Pacific Mail
                    steamship <hi rendition="#italic">City of New York</hi>, sent to him from me,
                    asked that the word “expiation “be stricken out and the word “explanation”
                    substituted. After a polite discussion I refused to comply with his request,
                    stating that the word “expiation” conveyed the precise meaning and literal sense
                    intended by me, and that it could not be changed without authority from my
                    Government.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Samuel Kimberly</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Chargé d’Affaires ad int</hi>.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
